The day the reboot news was announced, Glee star Kevin McHale &#8212; who played Artie Abrams on the show &#8212; took to X and quote-tweeted a Genius post from that fateful day in January 2020 that announced &#8220;drake has officially passed the cast of ‘glee’ and now has the most billboard hot 100 hits of all time.&#8221;

His simple response? &#8220;Challenge accepted.&#8221;

Challenge accepted https://t.co/LYvDU6KRRH

&mdash; Kevin McHale (@druidDUDE) October 21, 2026

The top responses harkened back to the heyday of both Twitter and Glee itself, with Tyler Oakley chiming in: &#8220;this being how i found out.&#8221; Meanwhile, Mike O&#8217;Malley &#8212; who played Kurt Hummel&#8217;s dad Burt on the original Fox series &#8212; asked, &#8220;Can I finally sing something now?!!!&#8221; Another commenter asked, &#8220;how long have you had this draft saved my god.&#8221;

Another quote-tweet marveled : &#8220;the concept of the glee cast re-assembling like the avengers just to dethrone drake.&#8221;

Of course, the Glee cast is going to have their work cut out for them, given more than a decade has transpired since the original series went off the air in 2015 and a lot more contenders have emerged in the years after. Since tying the Glee record of 207 Hot 100 entries in 2020, Drake now stands at a whopping 407. Not only that, but two other artists stand between the Glee cast and Drake: Taylor Swift with 281 and Future with 246.

Out of those 207 Hot 100 entries, the Glee cast scored three top 10 hits during the series&#8217; original 2009-2015 run: &#8220;Don&#8217;t Stop Believin'&#8221; in 2009 (No. 4 peak), &#8220;Loser Like Me&#8221; in 2011 (No. 6 peak), and &#8220;Teenage Dream&#8221; in 2010 (No. 8 peak).

The reboot news arrived first thing Wednesday, with the message &#8220;Currently gleeking out!&#8221; splashed across social media.
